Yep.  There's a reason for that: Successive minor-versions (the 'y' in
x.y.z) of Nethack have had substantial new features (for example, 3.1->3.2
provided a new interface for inventory selection operations which was
vastly superior to the one that existed in 3.1, and that is only the most
obvious change from 3.1 to 3.2), the majority of which are excellent and
well worth reincorporating into one's own variant, even at the cost of
non-trivial effort.

Also, the variants are more useful to the community if they keep pace
with the main stream of Nethack.  It lets the variant maintainer not
have to field dozens of "Hey, why can't I use $COOL_NETHACK_FEATURE in
your variant?" queries.

